WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:08.123
Every excitatory neuron of the mouse cortex is born at the bottom, in the zones

2
00:00:08.123 --> 00:00:09.283
beside the ventricle.

3
00:00:10.083 --> 00:00:11.583
So how do the layers get in order?

4
00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:15.893
Notch and Numb can make two sisters different at a division.

5
00:00:16.713 --> 00:00:19.813
But a yes or no alone cannot say which division it was.

6
00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:24.487
A stem cell that makes ten different daughters needs more than that.

7
00:00:25.127 --> 00:00:27.247
What tells the tenth daughter she is not the first?

8
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:31.883
Picture a slice through the wall of the embryonic mouse forebrain.

9
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.183
The ventricle is at the bottom, the pia on top.

10
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:38.887
The ventricular zone lines the ventricle.

11
00:00:39.587 --> 00:00:41.827
Many of its progenitors are radial glia.

12
00:00:42.627 --> 00:00:45.267
They divide and send a long fibre up to the pia.

13
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:48.627
Just above sits the subventricular zone.

14
00:00:49.887 --> 00:00:53.967
Intermediate progenitors, daughters of radial glia, divide there, usually to make

15
00:00:53.967 --> 00:00:54.967
neurons.

16
00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.770
The first neurons form a thin sheet just under the pia.

17
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.630
This is the preplate.

18
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.510
The next neurons arrive in the middle of the preplate and wedge it apart.

19
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:11.963
The top half becomes the marginal zone, future layer 1, home of the Cajal-Retzius

20
00:01:11.963 --> 00:01:14.963
cells. The bottom half becomes the subplate.

21
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:19.267
Between them, the cortical plate becomes layers 2 to 6.

22
00:01:20.207 --> 00:01:22.507
Each new neuron climbs up from below to reach it.

23
00:01:23.417 --> 00:01:26.737
Layers are numbered from the surface down, layer 1 on top.

24
00:01:27.597 --> 00:01:31.077
In the cortical plate, they are made the other way, from 6 up to 2.

25
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.807
The map raises three puzzles.

26
00:01:39.887 --> 00:01:40.887
First, who came first?

27
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:42.667
How can you tell?

28
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:44.937
Second, record or cause?

29
00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:49.597
Does the progenitor carry its own schedule, or do its surroundings decide?

30
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:52.007
Third, how many of each?

31
00:01:52.847 --> 00:01:57.287
How does one pool make a few neurons, then many, as it first grows and then shrinks?

32
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.620
Here is the one idea to carry.

33
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.720
A birthday marks a time.

34
00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.320
The progenitor's state at that time sets the neuron's temporal identity.

35
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.080
The birthday instructs nothing.

36
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.000
It is a timestamp.

37
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.980
In ferret grafts, laminar fate becomes fixed late in the final cell cycle.

38
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.600
Migration and later programs then build the layer.

39
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:27.573
That state has two parts: what the progenitor can still make, its competence, and

40
00:02:27.573 --> 00:02:28.573
the cues it reads.

41
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:33.447
In the cortical plate, the earlier the birthday, the deeper the layer.

42
00:02:34.307 --> 00:02:36.447
The neuron keeps maturing after it is born.

43
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.590
And in normal development, the progenitor's menu gets shorter with time.

44
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:51.873
The takeaway: cortical-plate neurons are born near the ventricle and climb.

45
00:02:52.953 --> 00:02:54.913
Layers 6 to 2 are made in that order.
